{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgAGAjbCKn6m9iMiMUsyW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`Hijack()` no longer fails just because the headers were already sent. It now refuses only after body bytes have been written. All tests pass and `go vet` is clean. I haven't committed anything.\n\n**The bug:** #4295 made `Hijack()` return an error whenever `Written()` was true. That covers two cases: body data already sent, which really can't be hijacked, and headers flushed with no body yet (`size == 0`), which is fine to hijack. Libraries like `github.com/coder/websocket` write headers and then hijack. So does middleware that calls `WriteHeaderNow()` or `Flush()` first. That second case was failing.\n\n**Changes:**\n- **`response_writer.go`:** `Hijack()` now checks `w.size > 0` instead of `w.Written()`, and has a comment explaining why. The error message now reads `\"gin: response body already written\"`.\n- **`response_writer_test.go`:** added cases to `TestResponseWriterHijackAfterWrite`:\n  - hijacking after `WriteHeader(101)` + `WriteHeaderNow()` succeeds\n  - hijacking after `Flush()` succeeds\n  - hijacking after `WriteString` fails with `errHijackAlreadyWritten`, alongside the existing `Write` case\n\nYou're on a detached HEAD. Want me to create a branch and commit this as `fix(response): refine hijack behavior for response lifecycle`?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1594,"cache_read_input_tokens":34250,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1594,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"295a6d70-1028-41da-8e60-ff5d515c58d6","uuid":"b18a6e20-0a1f-4546-a8b7-f169a89bd635","timestamp":"2026-10-18T19:34:14.546Z","request_id":"req_011CgAGAjPJAbeXxba2PFRkz"}
